.\" Manpage for whatever2sbom. __VERSION__ is substituted at package build time.
.TH WHATEVER2SBOM 1 "2026-06-17" "whatever2sbom __VERSION__" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
whatever2sbom \- generate a validated CycloneDX SBOM from a system or project
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B whatever2sbom
.RB [ options ]
.B \-\-product\-supplier
.I NAME
.RB [ "system-specific options" ]
.PP
.B whatever2sbom
.RB [ \-\-system
.IR SYSTEM ]
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B whatever2sbom
generates a validated Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) from the real, installed
state of an ecosystem rather than from a manifest of what was requested. It
collects packages for the selected
.BR \-\-system ,
normalises each into a common record, optionally enriches them (hashes,
licences, provenance), formats the result into the chosen
.B \-\-schema
document, and validates that document against the bundled schema before writing
it.
.PP
All schema and reference data (the CycloneDX JSON schema and the SPDX licence
list) are bundled with the program; no network access is performed at run time.
.PP
Schema validation is
.B always
performed and is fatal: if the generated document does not conform to the
schema, every error is printed to standard error, nothing is written, and the
program exits non-zero.
.PP
Installed from the Debian package,
.B whatever2sbom
runs from a self-contained virtual environment under
.IR /opt/whatever2sbom ;
the
.I /usr/bin/whatever2sbom
wrapper merely execs it, so it does not interfere with the host's system Python.
.SH OPTIONS
These global options apply to every system.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show a help message and exit. Only the selected system's options are listed
(see
.BR "SYSTEM-SPECIFIC OPTIONS" ).
.TP
.B \-\-system \fISYSTEM\fR
Ecosystem to scan: one of
.BR dpkg ", " npm ", or " pip
(default:
.BR dpkg ).
Selects which collector and enrichers run and which system-specific options
apply.
.TP
.B \-\-schema \fIFORMAT\fR
Output document schema (default:
.BR cyclonedx ).
.TP
.B \-\-spec\-version \fIVERSION\fR
Specification version of the chosen schema (default:
.BR 1.6 ).
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-output \fIFILE\fR
Write the SBOM to
.IR FILE .
The default is \fIsbom_<timestamp>.<ext>\fR, where the extension is fixed by the
schema
.RI ( .cdx.json
for CycloneDX).
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
Enable debug-level logging to standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-performance\-metrics
Print a per-stage timing breakdown (collect, enrich, format, validate, write) to
standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-bsi\-tr\-compliant
After writing the SBOM, additionally check it against the BSI TR-03183-2 v2.1.0
data-field requirements (SPDX-only licences, SHA-512 hashes, creator contact
information, the \fBbsi:component:*\fR properties). This check is advisory and
non-fatal: findings are summarised on standard error and written in full to
.IR <output>.bsi-report.txt .
.SH PRODUCT METADATA OPTIONS
These describe the product the SBOM is
.IR about ,
as opposed to the components found inside it. They populate
.B metadata.component
and
.BR metadata.supplier .
.TP
.B \-\-product\-supplier \fINAME\fR
Supplier / vendor name (NTIA Supplier Name).
.B Required
for every system.
.TP
.B \-\-product\-name \fINAME\fR
Name of the product or firmware image. Optional for
.B dpkg
(which can fall back to describing the host operating system); required for
.B npm
and
.BR pip ,
which have no host-OS fallback for
.BR metadata.component .
.TP
.B \-\-product\-version \fIVERSION\fR
Version of the product.
.TP
.B \-\-product\-type \fITYPE\fR
CycloneDX component type for the product
.RB ( firmware ", " application ", " container ", " device ", " operating-system ", ...)."
Default depends on
.BR \-\-system :
.B operating-system
for
.BR dpkg ,
.B application
for
.B pip
and
.BR npm .
.TP
.B \-\-product\-supplier\-url \fIURL\fR
Supplier URL. May be given multiple times.
.TP
.B \-\-product\-supplier\-email \fIEMAIL\fR
Supplier contact e-mail address. Satisfies the BSI TR-03183-2 creator-contact
requirement (sections 3.2.2 and 5.2.1).
.TP
.B \-\-product\-purl \fIPURL\fR
Package-URL uniquely identifying the product (e.g.
.IR pkg:generic/acme/fw@1.0 ).
When set, the product is also added as the root node of the dependency tree.
.TP
.B \-\-author \fI'Name <email>'\fR
SBOM author, in
.RI ' "Name <email>" '
form. May be given multiple times; populates
.BR metadata.authors .
.SH PLUGIN OPTIONS
Optional post-processing scripts that run last \(em after formatting, just
before schema validation \(em so their output is still validated.
.TP
.B \-\-plugin \fINAME\fR
Enable a plugin by script name (without
.IR .py ).
May be given multiple times; plugins run in the order listed.
.TP
.B \-\-plugin\-config \fINAME:KEY=VALUE\fR
Configure a plugin. May be given multiple times. A comma-separated
.I VALUE
becomes a list, e.g.
.IR \-\-plugin\-config\ patch-purl:packages=bash,coreutils .
.TP
.B \-\-plugin\-config\-file \fIFILE\fR
JSON file mapping plugin name to a config object. Merged
.I under
any inline
.B \-\-plugin\-config
values, which win on conflict.
.PP
One plugin ships built in:
.B patch-purl
rewrites the PURL namespace of named components (config:
.IR namespace ", " packages ).
.SH SYSTEM-SPECIFIC OPTIONS
Each system contributes its own options, listed below. Note that
.B \-\-help
displays only the options of the
.I selected
system (the default,
.BR dpkg ,
when
.B \-\-system
is omitted); run
.B whatever2sbom \-\-system
.I SYSTEM
.B \-\-help
to see another system's options.
.SS dpkg system options
Debian/Ubuntu packages via
.BR dpkg-query (1),
enriched from
.BR apt-cache (8)
and DEP-5 copyright files. This is the default system.
.PP
OSV and the Ubuntu/Debian security trackers key advisories on the
.I source
package with
.BR arch=source .
A package that is its own source (binary name equals the source name, e.g.
.BR bash ", " python3.12 )
therefore carries the
.B arch=source
coordinate, so its CVEs match. A binary built from a differently-named source
(e.g.
.B libpython3.12-stdlib
from source
.BR python3.12 )
carries its own binary coordinate plus an informational
.B upstream=\fIsource\fR
qualifier, so a shared source-level CVE is not reported once per binary. When a
source ships no binary of the same name (e.g.
.B nvidia-graphics-drivers-590
or
.BR linux-hwe-6.17 ),
a single logical
.B arch=source
component is added for that source so its advisories still match; it is marked
with the property
.B dpkg:source-pseudo-component
and validated as a logical node under
.BR \-\-bsi\-tr\-compliant .
.TP
.B \-\-distro \fIID\fR
Override the distribution identifier used in package PURLs (e.g.
.BR ubuntu ).
Auto-detected from
.I /etc/os-release
when omitted.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-apt\-cache
Skip
.BR apt-cache (8)
hash and filename enrichment. Hashes and download metadata are then absent for
most packages.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-licenses
Skip licence extraction from
.IR /usr/share/doc/ <pkg> /copyright .
The
.B licenses
field is then empty on all components.
.TP
.B \-\-exclude \fIPATTERN\fR
Exclude an installed package from the SBOM. \fIPATTERN\fR is an exact package
name, or a glob when it contains
.BR * ", " ? ", or " [...]
(e.g.
.BR linux-image-* ", " *-dbg ).
Matching is case-sensitive, against the bare package name. Accepts a
comma-separated list (e.g.
.IR linux-libc-dev,*-dev ),
may be given multiple times, and is merged with
.BR \-\-exclude\-file .
Exclusion happens before the dependency graph and synthetic source components
are built; a pattern matching no installed package is reported on stderr.
.TP
.B \-\-exclude\-file \fIFILE\fR
Read exclusion patterns from
.IR FILE ,
one name or glob per line. Blank lines and
.B #
comments (whole-line or trailing) are ignored. Merged with any
.B \-\-exclude
values.
.SS npm system options
Node.js packages resolved from an npm
.IR package-lock.json .
Reads the lockfile only; no network access and no call to
.BR npm (1).
.TP
.B \-\-lockfile \fIPATH\fR
Path to
.IR package-lock.json ,
or a directory to search (default: current directory; tries
.I package-lock.json
then npm's hidden
.IR node_modules/.package-lock.json ).
Only
.B lockfileVersion
2 and 3 (npm >= 7) are supported.
.TP
.B \-\-exclude\-dev\-dependencies
Omit devDependencies (lockfile entries marked
.B dev
or
.BR devOptional )
and any dependency edges pointing at them. Without this flag, such packages are
emitted with CycloneDX scope
.BR excluded .
.SS pip system options
Python packages installed in a virtualenv, read via
.BR importlib.metadata .
.TP
.B \-\-venv\-dir \fIPATH\fR
Path to the virtualenv to scan; it must contain
.IR pyvenv.cfg .
.B $VIRTUAL_ENV
is deliberately not consulted, so the tool's own environment cannot shadow the
target project's.
.TP
.B \-\-project\-dir \fIPATH\fR
Project root under which to auto-detect a virtualenv (a directory containing
.IR pyvenv.cfg )
when
.B \-\-venv\-dir
is not given (default: current directory).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The SBOM was written successfully. A non-empty
.B \-\-bsi\-tr\-compliant
report does
.I not
change this.
.TP
.B 1
A configuration error, a plugin error, a schema-validation failure, or a runtime
error during collection, enrichment, or formatting.
.TP
.B 2
A command-line usage error (unknown or invalid option, or a missing required
option such as
.BR \-\-product\-supplier ).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /opt/whatever2sbom/venv
Self-contained virtual environment the program runs from, created on package
installation.
.TP
.I /opt/whatever2sbom/wheels
Bundled dependency wheels used to (re)install the program fully offline.
.TP
.I /opt/whatever2sbom/plugins
System-wide drop-in directory searched for
.B \-\-plugin
scripts.
.TP
.I /etc/os-release
Read by
.B \-\-system dpkg
for the distribution identifier and codename used in PURLs and the host-OS
fallback component.
.TP
.I sbom_<timestamp>.<ext>
Default output file when
.B \-o
is not given.
.TP
.I <output>.bsi-report.txt
Full BSI TR-03183-2 findings, written when
.B \-\-bsi\-tr\-compliant
reports any.
.SH EXAMPLES
Scan the local Debian/Ubuntu system (default):
.RS 4
.EX
whatever2sbom \-\-product\-supplier "Acme GmbH"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Scan a Node.js project and name the product:
.RS 4
.EX
whatever2sbom \-\-system npm \-\-lockfile ./app \\
              \-\-product\-name app \-\-product\-supplier "Acme GmbH"
.EE
.RE
.PP
Scan a Python virtualenv and run the BSI compliance check:
.RS 4
.EX
whatever2sbom \-\-system pip \-\-venv\-dir ./.venv \\
              \-\-product\-name app \-\-product\-supplier "Acme GmbH" \-\-bsi\-tr\-compliant
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dpkg-query (1),
.BR apt-cache (8).
.PP
CycloneDX specification: https://cyclonedx.org/
.br
BSI TR-03183-2: https://www.bsi.bund.de/
.br
Full documentation: https://teeque87.github.io/whatever2sbom/
.SH AUTHOR
Michael Hansonis <whatever2sbom@teeque.dev>.
